WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.560
Agents are powerful, but they're

2
00:00:01.839 --> 00:00:06.879
unreliable and overconfident. They rush

3
00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:09.599
to start coding and waste time building

4
00:00:06.879 --> 00:00:11.440
the wrong thing. They claim success when

5
00:00:09.599 --> 00:00:14.639
they're clearly not done. They

6
00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:16.800
hallucinate APIs and libraries, and when

7
00:00:14.639 --> 00:00:19.520
they add one feature, they break three

8
00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:21.680
others. Using agents means endless

9
00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:24.720
reviewing and frustrating attempts to

10
00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:26.160
correct mistakes after the fact. Tesla

11
00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:29.039
solves this with specd-driven

12
00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:31.599
development, keeping AI agents on rails

13
00:00:29.039 --> 00:00:33.840
and well informed. It gives you the ease

14
00:00:31.599 --> 00:00:36.239
of agent decoding with the quality of

15
00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:38.640
production software. The Tesla framework

16
00:00:36.239 --> 00:00:41.280
integrates with your agent and makes it

17
00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:44.079
define the product features in a spec

18
00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:46.480
before writing the code. Each spec

19
00:00:44.079 --> 00:00:48.879
contains tests that verify the code

20
00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:51.520
meets the requirements, so the agent can

21
00:00:48.879 --> 00:00:53.920
keep iterating until it gets it right.

22
00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:56.160
Specs don't need to slow you down. You

23
00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:58.640
can choose to review them before

24
00:00:56.160 --> 00:01:01.680
building the code to ensure your intent

25
00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:03.680
was well captured, or you can vibe spec

26
00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:05.600
and let the agent flow from spec to

27
00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:08.000
code, only checking the specs if

28
00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:10.479
something goes wrong. Either way, the

29
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:12.720
specs become part of your codebase and

30
00:01:10.479 --> 00:01:15.600
serve as long-term memory of what your

31
00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:17.520
product should do. Agents can evolve the

32
00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:19.280
product better this way, and tests

33
00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:21.200
ensure that they don't break what's

34
00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:23.759
already working.

35
00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:26.000
Tesla's spec registry helps agents use

36
00:01:23.759 --> 00:01:28.400
open source software better. It includes

37
00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:30.880
over 10,000 usage specs for popular

38
00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:33.520
libraries, preventing API hallucinations

39
00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:35.280
and version confusion. Usage specs are

40
00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:37.200
packaged and versioned just like

41
00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:40.000
software modules, and you can publish

42
00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:42.240
your own custom context to the registry.

43
00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:45.119
Together, the framework and registry

44
00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:46.960
ensure your agents know what and how you

45
00:01:45.119 --> 00:01:48.640
want them to build. Specdriven

46
00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:51.439
development is the way to build software

47
00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:53.840
in the AI era. It makes agents better

48
00:01:51.439 --> 00:01:56.079
with little extra effort and unlocks

49
00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:58.560
their use in professional code bases.

50
00:01:56.079 --> 00:02:01.360
The Tesla framework and registry are now

51
00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:05.079
available in beta. Try them out. I can't

52
00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:05.079
wait to see what you build.
